  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nIncoming Heavy Rainfall Overnight & Through Friday:\n\nThe area of higher pressure and accompanying anticyclonic flow \nwill slowly exit east/southeast through the end of the work week\nas a deepening mid/upper level perturbation ushers in heavy \nrainfall potential across the Upper Midwest overnight Thursday \nthrough Friday. The responsible upper level jet streak seen on \nGOES18 derived wind loops exhibits slight troughing will \nstrengthen as it circumnavigates the ridge periphery. Resultant \neasterly advection of a very moist, with a daily maximum PWAT of\n1.63\" observed at International Falls, MN 30.00Z RAOB to 90th \npercentile at Omaha, NE (SPC Climatology), traverses through the\nUpper Mississippi River Valley. Accompanying LREF (EPS/GEFS) \n100% probabilities for 1.5\" to 2\" PWATs bifurcate and graze the \nlocal forecast area, respectively, through Friday night. \n\nHeavy Rainfall Impacts:\n\nSimilar to previous forecasts, main differences between LREF \nmembers (EPS/GEFS) surround location and strength of the \ndeepening extratropical cyclone. A slightly more northern \nsolution between EPS members results in higher member consensus \nfor heavier precipitation amounts compared to the GEFS. Current \nforecast confidence for 1\" to 2\" of rainfall through Friday but \nshould the more northern, deeper solution come to fruition \nhigher amounts will need to be accounted for in the forecast. \n\nTiming:\n\nWhile confidence is high for initial precipitation to reach the\nlocal forecast area overnight into Friday morning, exit \nbehavior and subsequent duration remains additional forecast \ndetail to remain cognizant of. The stronger EPS solution \nsuggests lingering low level frontogenetical bands within the \ndeformation zone perpetuating precipitation through Saturday. \nCurrent LREF cluster analysis confidence keeps PoPs abated \nthrough Saturday afternoon although additional scrutiny may need\nto be spent on Saturday as forecast hour nears should heavy \nrainfall impacts persist.\n\nSevere Storm Potential:\n\nWhile overall severe storm potential appears low (<5%), \nrelatively close proximity to the surface low amplifies low \nlevel vorticity maxima raising some concern and questions \nsurrounding tornado potential should surface stretching occur. \n\nSunday - Tuesday: Seasonable Temperatures and Mostly Dry\n\nBehind the late week showers and storms, synoptic scale ridging \nbuilds across the central United States and remains in place\nthrough early next week with seasonable temperatures expected \nin response. Stability associated with this ridge looks to keep\nshowers/storms at bay through at least Tuesday when ensemble \nguidance suggests a shortwave at 500hPa shifts eastward across \nthe United States/Canada border, forcing a weak surface \nboundary through the Upper Midwest. Precipitation is possible \nlate Tuesday along this boundary, though variations amongst the \nvarious ensemble suites keeps probabilities on the lower end for\nthe time being, sitting around 30-40% in the 29.12z LREF.",
